README.md:12-13 and the SYNOPSIS of lib/Crypt/Age.pm present both example keys as the return of a single generate_keypair() call. Each string is valid Bech32 on its own -- that was the 35e4a03 fix -- but they do not belong together.

Verified: deriving the public key from the documented secret key
  AGE-SECRET-KEY-1QQQ...8H00W3
yields
  age19ljhmg68e43yx9fgm2k9lwefquc0la5y4lzvlshdjzv47kxt8d6qr9vf4p
while the documented public key is
  age1ql3z7hjy54pw3hyww5ayyfg7zqgvc7w3j2elw8zmrj2kg5sfn9aqmcac8p

Anyone copying both out of the SYNOPSIS to try encrypt/decrypt against each other gets "No matching identity found" and has no way to tell the example is at fault.

Wanted: replace the public key with the one actually derived from the documented secret key, in both README.md and the Crypt::Age SYNOPSIS. Keep the all-Q secret key -- its shape marks it as a placeholder rather than a usable identity, which is the right signal for documentation.
Fixed in the working tree: SYNOPSIS and KEY FORMAT in both README.md and lib/Crypt/Age.pm now carry the public key actually derived from the documented secret key (age19ljhmg...). Verified: the old mismatched key is gone from the whole tree, and encrypt/decrypt with exactly the documented values round-trips.
